\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsubsection}{Apportionment of the two timing budgets}{118}{subsubsection*.134}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {37}{\ignorespaces Apportionment of the budgets of Equations~\ref {eq:sys-cls} and~\ref {eq:sys-rep} across the stages, with the laptop figures of Section~\ref {sec:results} against each. No stage was timed on the single-board computer, so every verdict here is provisional on hardware that is faster than the target. The two stages that fail are the two that were never redesigned after first being written: page segmentation and the physical writing.}}{118}{table.caption.135}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:app-budget}{{37}{118}{Apportionment of the budgets of Equations~\ref {eq:sys-cls} and~\ref {eq:sys-rep} across the stages, with the laptop figures of Section~\ref {sec:results} against each. No stage was timed on the single-board computer, so every verdict here is provisional on hardware that is faster than the target. The two stages that fail are the two that were never redesigned after first being written: page segmentation and the physical writing}{table.caption.135}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsubsection}{Pseudocode of the emitting loop and the start-up warm-up}{119}{subsubsection*.136}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {48}{\ignorespaces The emitting loop of the G-code generator as pseudocode. \texttt  {SetPen} returns nothing at all when the pen is already in the wanted state, which is what keeps the one-way toggle motor of Section~\ref {sec:gan-pen} in step with the program's model of it; the dwell it emits on a change is the $260+90$\,ms of Table~\ref {tab:gc-config}. Fault~2 of Section~\ref {sec:gc-faults} is visible here: the draw feed is emitted before every stroke but the travel feed only once, so every travel after the first runs at the draw feed.}}{119}{figure.caption.137}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:gc-emitpseudo}{{48}{119}{The emitting loop of the G-code generator as pseudocode. \texttt {SetPen} returns nothing at all when the pen is already in the wanted state, which is what keeps the one-way toggle motor of Section~\ref {sec:gan-pen} in step with the program's model of it; the dwell it emits on a change is the $260+90$\,ms of Table~\ref {tab:gc-config}. Fault~2 of Section~\ref {sec:gc-faults} is visible here: the draw feed is emitted before every stroke but the travel feed only once, so every travel after the first runs at the draw feed}{figure.caption.137}{}}
\ttl@finishall
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {49}{\ignorespaces The start-up warm-up as pseudocode. The two cache-building calls are the ones that, on a miss, reach into the retired source tree and import a machine-learning library; because three writers have no cached files as delivered, that miss happens on every start, which is the defect Section~\ref {sec:system} records. Running it on a background thread is what keeps the delay off the first request, and marking each writer separately is what lets the routes that do not need a cache answer immediately.}}{120}{figure.caption.138}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:sys-code-warm}{{49}{120}{The start-up warm-up as pseudocode. The two cache-building calls are the ones that, on a miss, reach into the retired source tree and import a machine-learning library; because three writers have no cached files as delivered, that miss happens on every start, which is the defect Section~\ref {sec:system} records. Running it on a background thread is what keeps the delay off the first request, and marking each writer separately is what lets the routes that do not need a cache answer immediately}{figure.caption.138}{}}
